# OSM route overlay (dev) — direct route traversal

A dev-only visual + interaction layer that draws the walkable subset of the OSM graph as a flat, translucent **glass-tinted route mesh** floating just above the world, then lets the user **point-and-click along the routes** to walk the player on them — bypassing the NavMesh / `RouteComposer` entirely. Adds a **Play/Pause floating button** that auto-advances the player along the route they're on until the next junction or dead-end.

This is **not** the City Navigate widget (`osm-navigation.mdc`). That widget renders a single calculated route for a chosen POI in bird's-eye view. The overlay here renders the **whole walkable graph** in third/first-person and treats every visible segment as a directly-walkable surface — *"the route IS the path"*.

> **Naming**: The feature was originally prototyped as "ribbon overlay" (after the visual style — wide flat translucent strip). The user asked to rename it to something more descriptive; we standardised on **OSM route overlay**. The token `OsmRoute*` (with the disambiguating `Overlay` suffix on events/components) is consistent with the `osm-navigation` widget's `osmRoute*` events and clear about what the surface represents — the walkable OSM route network.

**Service**: `younite.navmesh_route_extension/services/kit_services/osm_route_overlay/` — `OsmRouteOverlayService` in `service.py` (import from `osm_route_overlay` package root re-exports the class)
**Wired in**: `younite.navmesh_route_extension/extension.py` (constructed with the shared `NavMeshModeCache` + `NavMeshRouteService`, started/stopped with the extension)
**Depends on**: `younite.osm_navigation_extension` (graph data), `younite.messaging_core_extension` (event bridge), `younite.usd_viewer_player_core_extension` (auto-mover)

---

## Visual model — translucent glass

A single `UsdGeom.Mesh` of horizontal quads (one quad per visible OSM edge) authored on the **session layer** under `/World/OsmRouteDevOverlay/Walking`, with `UsdPhysics.CollisionAPI` + `MeshCollisionAPI` so PhysX raycasts hit the route surface directly.

**Material** (`UsdPreviewSurface` at `OVERLAY_MATERIAL_PATH = .../Looks/RouteMaterial`):

| Input | Value | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `inputs:diffuseColor` | `OVERLAY_COLOR = (0.32, 0.62, 0.95)` | Cool tinted blue, slightly desaturated so it reads as tinted glass not paint stripe |
| `inputs:opacity` | `OVERLAY_OPACITY = 0.22` | True see-through. Stays visible over bright pavement; below 0.18 it disappears, above 0.30 it starts reading opaque again |
| `inputs:opacityThreshold` | **`0.0` (explicit)** | **Required** — RTX defaults to alpha-cutout (binary masking) and renders fully opaque matte unless this is explicitly authored to 0. Same recipe `rain_service` uses |
| `inputs:emissiveColor` | `OVERLAY_COLOR × OVERLAY_EMISSIVE_GAIN = 0.06` | Tiny boost so glass reads in deep shadow. Emissive in `UsdPreviewSurface` is **not modulated by opacity** — high values dominate the pixel and read as matte regardless of alpha (this was the original "smoky-grey matte" bug) |
| `inputs:roughness` | `OVERLAY_ROUGHNESS = 0.18` | Sharp specular for glass-like sheen. >0.5 reads as chalk/matte |
| `inputs:metallic` | `0.0` | Dielectric reflection, not chrome stripe |
| Mesh `subdivisionScheme` | `"none"` | Flat quads, no Catmull-Clark — subdiv routes through a different RTX shader path that drops translucency |
| Mesh `displayColor` | **not authored** | RTX's gprim sync uses `displayColor` as surface albedo and **bypasses** the bound translucent material when authored. The original "Hydra Storm fallback" was actually fighting the real material — never re-add it |

**Geometry constants** (in service file, all in cm):

- `OVERLAY_HOVER_CM = 35.0` — above the player's CCT capsule top (~22 cm) but below CCT `stepOffset` (40 cm), so any rare contact resolves via the auto-step rather than getting stuck.
- `OVERLAY_WIDTH_CM = 110.0` — comfortable mobile touch target without flooding narrow alleys.

**Visible classes** — `VISUAL_EDGE_CLASSES = {pedestrian, steps, residential, arterial, service}`. Showing only `pedestrian` + `steps` hid sidewalks down residential streets that the router happily uses, leaving "phantom" gaps the user couldn't tap. `_parse_osm_edge` still rejects mode-impassable edges via `OsmGraphService._edge_cost(...)`, so `wheelchair` mode hides `steps` automatically — the on-screen routes match exactly what Dijkstra would consider.

