## Events

| Event | Direction | Payload | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `osmRouteOverlaySet` | Web → Kit | `{ visible: bool, roadSnap?: bool }` | Build / tear down the route mesh + visible subgraph |
| `osmRouteOverlayStatus` | Kit → Web | `{ visible: bool }` | Confirms overlay state |
| `osmRouteOverlayAttached` | Kit → Web | `{ attached: bool }` | Player↔route binding flips. Drives FAB visibility. Sticky: stays `true` between successful click-walks and after arrival, flips to `false` only on a NavMesh point-click (jump-off) or overlay teardown. **Idempotent on the Kit side** — it doesn't re-emit when the value didn't change, so the long stream of NavMesh-only clicks made before the overlay is ever used is silent |
| `younite.osm_route_overlay.click` | Kit internal | `{ world: {x,y,z}, pickPrimPath }` | Click landed on or near the visible overlay |
| `younite.navigation.requestPoint` | Kit internal | `{ world: {x,y,z}, pickPrimPath? }` | NavMesh point-click. The overlay service **subscribes** to this purely so it can detach when the user jumps off; the orchestrator owns the actual NavMesh routing |
| `osmRouteOverlayAutoMove` | Web → Kit | `{ action: "play" \| "stop" }` | Toggle the auto-advance walk |
| `osmRouteOverlayMarker` (intent) | Web → Kit | (sent via `younite.pick.request`) | Forces the click router to treat the pick as an OSM-overlay click even when the web's overlay-active flag is out of sync |
| `navmeshRouteWaypoints` | Kit → auto-mover | `{ routeId: "player", waypoints, segmentSpeeds, faceDirection? }` | Direct polyline dispatch (bypasses orchestrator + composer) |
| `navigationStateSet` | Kit → web/auto-mover | `{ movementMode: "pointClick", autoMove: true, routeId: "player", ... }` | Prime auto-mover before dispatching waypoints |
| `autoMoveStatus` | Kit → Web | `{ routeId, active, reason }` | Drives the FAB Play/Pause icon when `routeId === "player"` |

> **Disambiguation from City Navigate**: `osm-navigation` uses `osmRouteResult` / `osmRouteInfo` for the calculated single-POI route. The overlay events all carry the `Overlay` suffix (`osmRouteOverlay*`) to keep them distinct.

